From: Gran Turismo 6 for Mac <https://github.com/Wooloomooloo2/Gran-Turismo-6-Mac>
Subject: [PATCH] SPURS: unlifted job binaries and policy modules can run interpreted; synthetic job events can be turned off

Upstream: not yet submitted
Base: ps3recomp 4e8f3ca87a3e1d87bb83b8b7e9348fe2685cd514 (after 0001-0007)

What: two opt-in switches, defaults unchanged.
- SPU_JOB_INTERP=1: a SPURS job binary with no lifted image
  (spu_workload_dispatch_job) and a workload policy module with no lifted
  image (cellSpurs.c spurs_resolve_pm) run in the SPU interpreter through
  spu_dispatch, with the same staging as lifted code (jm2 job context at LS 0;
  kernel context, PM at LS 0xA00), instead of being dropped. Interpreted
  images get image ids no lifted image uses. Every run is reported to a new
  hook, g_spu_job_interp_hook (fingerprint, size), which a port can count;
  the first 32 job runs and every 10000th are logged. SPU_DUMP_MISS still
  dumps each image once; policy modules now dump as spupm_<fp>_<size>.bin
  (linked for LS 0xA00) so a lift can tell them from spujob_ (LS 0).
- SPURS_JOB_SYNTH_EVENTS=0: the job-chain walker stops making up completion
  events (one per job, one at END, one at the walk's end, pushed to every
  attached queue). On hardware the job manager sends none; a job reports its
  end itself, through its own SPU user event (already routed by
  spurs_port_queue) or memory.

Why: Gran Turismo 6 runs at least 16 job binaries that are not lifted and its
Wws_Job policy module. Dropped, their work never happens, and the made-up
events (data2 = job index) are read by GT6's event-queue worker as its own
callback indices. Its first-run install then decompresses data no job
produced, overruns a static buffer into the USB driver's state, and exits
when a mutex lock on the clobbered id fails.

Test: tools/test_spu_job_interp.py (new): without the switch the job is
dropped; with it, the job runs interpreted (writes its mailbox), the hook
sees it, and SPU_DUMP_MISS dumps it once. tools/test_spu_dispatch_miss.py
unchanged and passing.

